    {
      "id": "hagelberg-hirschfeld",
      "nationId": "prussians-1815",
      "name": "Hirschfeld's Corps",
      "formation": "Detached corps, Army of the North",
      "side": "Prussian",
      "summary": "Hirschfeld's column of Kurmark Landwehr, raw troops of a few weeks' service with only a stiffening of the 1st Reserve Regiment, which caught Girard round Hagelberg. Its battalions stormed the village, and when their wet muskets would not fire they fought it out at the walls with butts and bayonets; Chernyshev's Cossacks came up to complete the rout.",
      "notes": [
        "Built from Albion Triumphant's Prussian 1815 list, the nearest to 1813: its Prussian Infantry Brigade is the 1813 brigade, the size of another army's division, with line, reserve and Landwehr battalions.",
        "After Nafziger's order of battle for Hagelberg.",
        "Battalions of different regiments that served together are grouped as a regiment, as the list takes them. Reserve battalions are the list's Musketeers; grenadier battalions are Musketeers with its Veteran upgrade.",
        "Brigades of three regiments or more have a Subordinate Commander, named where the order of battle names the brigade's infantry commander.",
        "The Kurmark Landwehr had been raised that summer and were the list's Landwehr to the life, Unreliable rule and all.",
        "Hirschfeld's brigades were wings and detachments of a few battalions each, not the list's brigades: Langen's three 1st Reserve battalions with no Landwehr, Marwitz's one Landwehr regiment and Boguslawski's two with no line regiment, Landwehr regiments of one or four battalions, and Reuss's battery with three battalions to support it all show as historical departures.",
        "The 3rd Kurmark Landwehr Cavalry's fourth squadron rode with Reuss's detachment, as its attached Landwehr cavalry.",
        "Commanders are at Staff Rating 8, the default for historical forces."
      ],
